1- User opens their browser, enters "www.foobar.com" into their search box
2- Browser asks DNS to switch it to an IP address
3- DNS responds with 8.8.8.8 (this time 8.8.8.8 is the load balancer IP)
4- After Browser gets it, it sends HTTP(S) request to that address (on port 80)
5- Request reaches the load balancer server (machine with IP 8.8.8.8 running HAProxy)
6- Load balancer (HAProxy) looks at its config and picks one of the 2 backend servers (for example using Round Robin: first request to Server 1, second to Server 2, third back to Server 1, etc..)
7- HAProxy forwards the HTTP(S) request to the chosen backend server (Server 1 or Server 2)
8- On that backend server (its own private IP), the web server (Nginx) receives the request first
9- Nginx checks: If it’s a normal static file (files stored as-is on disk, ex: basic HTML files, .png, .jpg, .css, .js), Nginx sends it directly back to HAProxy
10- Nginx checks: If it’s a non-static/dynamic request, it sends it to the application server on the same machine to process it and return the response back to Nginx
11- Application server may use the local MySQL database to get/store data (one server is configured as Primary DB, the other as Replica)
12- Primary DB gets all the writes; Replica DB gets updates from the Primary through replication
13- Application server sends back HTTP(S) response to Nginx
14- Nginx sends the response back to HAProxy
15- Finally, HAProxy sends it back to the user browser

What is the load balancer (HAProxy) and why am I adding it
- A separate server in front of the 2 backend servers that receives all traffic and spreads requests between them so more users can be handled and if one backend dies the other can still serve the site
What are the 2 backend servers and why am I adding them
- Each backend server has Nginx + app server + app code + MySQL, so traffic doesn’t depend on a single machine anymore and we can handle more load
What distribution algorithm is the load balancer using and how it works
- Round Robin: it sends the first request to Server 1, second to Server 2, third to Server 1, and so on in a loop, which spreads traffic evenly
Is the load balancer enabling Active-Active or Active-Passive, and what is the difference
- Here it’s Active-Active on the backend: both Server 1 and Server 2 are active at the same time and receive traffic
- Active-Active: multiple servers are serving users at the same time, traffic is shared
- Active-Passive: one server is active, the other is on standby and only takes over if the active one fails
How a database Primary & Replica (Master & Slave) work
- Primary (Master) handles all the writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE)
- Replica (Slave) gets a copy of all changes from the Primary using replication (it replays the Primary’s changes). Usually as backup
What is the difference between the Primary node and the Replica node in regard to the application
- Primary: main DB the app writes to (and usually reads from), single source of truth
- Replica: usually used for reads or as backup; it does not accept writes from the app in a simple setup, it just follows the Primary
What is the role of the web server (on each backend server)
- Receives HTTP(S) request from the load balancer, returns static files if available, and sends non-static requests to the application server
What is the role of the application server (on each backend server)
- Handles backend code, business logic, and talks to the database when needed
What is the role of the database (on each backend server)
- Stores data; one acts as Primary DB, the other as Replica DB that copies data from the Primary
What is the server using to communicate with the computer of the user requesting the website
- It uses HTTP(S) over TCP/IP, but now the user talks first to the load balancer, and the load balancer talks to the backend servers

Issues with this infrastructure:
SPOF:
- Load balancer is a Single Point Of Failure – if HAProxy server (8.8.8.8) dies, no one can reach any backend server even if they are fine
- Primary database is also a SPOF for writes – if the Primary DB dies, the app can’t write data until a failover/promotion(Replica becomes Primary) happens
Security issues (no firewall, no HTTPS):
- No firewall: everything is exposed, no filtering of which IPs/ports can talk to which servers
- No HTTPS: traffic is not encrypted, data like passwords/cookies can be sniffed or modified
No monitoring:
- No system watching if servers, load balancer, or databases are up/down
- No alerts, no metrics (CPU, RAM, QPS, errors) so issues can happen and no one will notice quickly
